Based on my review of the panoramic radiograph, I examined each tooth's periapical region at magnification. The clearest radiolucencies I can identify are in the mandibular molar regions.

{"lesions": [{"box": [0.224, 0.515, 0.293, 0.635], "pai": 4}, {"box": [0.619, 0.551, 0.679, 0.671], "pai": 4}]}

Notes on confidence: this is a panoramic image, where periapical detail is limited by overlapping structures, so apical lesions are difficult to grade reliably (PAI was designed for intraoral periapical films). The two regions I flagged show well-defined radiolucent areas associated with the mandibular molars. I did not find clearly scoreable apical lesions in the maxillary molar apices (lucencies there were consistent with the maxillary sinus) or the anterior teeth, and several other dark areas I inspected were within tooth crowns (carious/restorative), not periapical.
